    essay: {
      visual_description: `<p>Harley MS 3469 replicates the Berlin archetype closely but with subtle stylistic differences suggesting a distinct Flemish or German atelier working fifty years later. The architectural borders remain elaborate but show a more Mannerist character; the figure work is slightly harder in line. The 22 miniatures follow the same sequence as Berlin: introductory workshop plate, the planetary series, seven flask plates, and the final solar king. The palette is equally rich, employing shell gold throughout and lapis lazuli for sky areas.</p>`,
      historical_context: `<p>The 1582 date of the Harley copy places it firmly in the Elizabethan alchemical milieu; the manuscript may have been produced on the Continent for an English patron. It entered the Harleian Library in the early eighteenth century and passed with the Harley collection to the British Museum in 1753. It was one of the first alchemical manuscripts to receive systematic scholarly attention, noted by Elias Ashmole in <em>Theatrum Chemicum Britannicum</em> (1652).</p>`,
      provenance: `<p>Harley MS 3469: acquired by Robert Harley, 1st Earl of Oxford (1661–1724) and his son Edward Harley. Bequeathed with the Harleian collection to the nation; now British Library. Fully digitized and freely accessible via the British Library Digitised Manuscripts portal.</p>`,
      alchemical_processes: `<p>As with the Berlin exemplar, the Harley Splendor Solis traces the sevenfold planetary-metallic opus from nigredo to rubedo. The green lion devouring the sun (f. 17r) is one of the most iconic images in alchemical iconography, representing the action of vitriol (green vitriol / ferrous sulphate) on gold during dissolution — or more esoterically, the capacity of raw chaotic nature to absorb and transform the highest principle.</p>`,
      scholarly_discussion: `<p>The Harley copy is the primary source for the Thames & Hudson facsimile edition (2019) with commentary by Stephen Skinner. Stanton Linden discusses its reception in English alchemical literature; Jennifer Rampling has used it as a case study in her work on material alchemy vs. spiritual allegory. The British Library's online catalogue entry includes full bibliographic history and a link to the digitized manuscript.</p>`
    },
