    status: "in_corpus",
    priority: "medium",
    symbol: "⚘",
    card_description: "Francesco Colonna's 1499 dream-romance, printed by Aldus Manutius with 172 exceptional woodcuts, is a key source for Renaissance hieroglyphic symbolism absorbed into alchemical emblem books. Its triumphs, gardens, and arcane architectural monuments were repeatedly referenced by Maier, Fludd, and the emblem tradition.",
    related: ["maier-atalanta", "fludd-utriusque"],
    images: [
      { id: "hypn-triumph", label: "Triumph of Cupid", folio_or_plate: "sig. e4r", thumb_description: "Elaborate triumphal procession with elephant, obelisk, and allegorical figures." },
      { id: "hypn-bath", label: "Nymph Bath", folio_or_plate: "sig. g2r", thumb_description: "Five nymphs bathing in a fountain basin surrounded by dense foliage." },
      { id: "hypn-labyrinth", label: "Garden Labyrinth", folio_or_plate: "sig. b3v", thumb_description: "Aerial-view woodcut of an elaborate hedged labyrinth, geometric pattern." }
    ],
    essay: {
      visual_description: `<p>The 172 woodcuts of the Hypnerotomachia represent the most sophisticated woodcut programme of the Italian incunabulum period. The illustrations range from elaborate architectural fantasies — impossible temples, triumphal arches with pseudo-Egyptian hieroglyphs, colossal sleeping figures — to intimate garden scenes featuring topiary, fountains, nymph baths, and ritual processions. The woodcut style is characterized by fine parallel hatching, aerial perspective in landscape scenes, and an unusual command of spatial depth rare in 1490s woodcut.</p>`,
      historical_context: `<p>The Hypnerotomachia's significance for alchemy lies primarily in its role as a visual encyclopaedia of Hermetic symbolism in Renaissance dress. Its pseudo-hieroglyphs, drawn from Horapollo and Valerian, were taken as genuine ancient wisdom by later emblem book authors. Michael Maier in <em>Atalanta Fugiens</em> (1618) repeatedly references Colonna's dream architecture; the garden of Venus in the Hypnerotomachia closely parallels the hortus philosophicus of many alchemical texts. The 1546 French translation introduced the imagery to a northern European audience that would produce the great alchemical emblem books of the early seventeenth century.</p>`,
      provenance: `<p>The 1499 Aldine edition survives in numerous copies; a second edition appeared in Venice in 1545 and a French translation by Jean Martin in Paris, 1546 and 1561. The woodblocks were likely cut in Venice, possibly after designs by Benedetto Bordone. The Internet Archive provides a scanned copy of the 1499 edition (call number gri_33125008447027).</p>`,
      alchemical_processes: `<p>The Hypnerotomachia is not an alchemical text proper, but its imagery of transformation — the sleeping figure of Poliphilo as prima materia, the garden as the alchemical vessel, the nymph baths as dissolution, the temple of Venus as the completed work — was read allegorically by later alchemists. Joscelyn Godwin's commentary on the Hypnerotomachia (Thames & Hudson, 1999) explores these Hermetic resonances systematically.</p>`,
      scholarly_discussion: `<p>Godwin, Joscelyn. <em>The Real Rule of Four</em> (2004) and his annotated translation of the Hypnerotomachia (Thames & Hudson, 1999) are the primary English-language resources. Liane Lefaivre's <em>Leon Battista Alberti's Hypnerotomachia Poliphili</em> (MIT Press, 1997) provides architectural analysis. For alchemical reception specifically, Urszula Szulakowska's <em>The Alchemy of Light</em> traces how the dream-garden imagery merged with Paracelsian cosmology in the emblem tradition.</p>`
    },
    bibliography: [
      { citation: "Colonna, Francesco. Hypnerotomachia Poliphili. Venice: Aldus Manutius, 1499. (Facsimile: Olms, 1980.)", url: "" },
      { citation: "Godwin, Joscelyn, trans. Hypnerotomachia Poliphili. London: Thames & Hudson, 1999.", url: "" },
      { citation: "Lefaivre, Liane. Leon Battista Alberti's Hypnerotomachia Poliphili. Cambridge, MA: MIT Press, 1997.", url: "" }
    ],
    links: [
      { label: "Internet Archive (1499 ed.)", url: "https://archive.org/details/hypnerotomachiap00colo", description: "Free full digital scan of the 1499 Aldine edition" },
      { label: "Getty Research Institute copy", url: "https://archive.org/details/gri_33125008447027", description: "High-resolution scan via Getty / Internet Archive" }
    ]
